    bio: "Tycho Brahe was born into Danish nobility in 1546 and should, by every expectation of his class, have spent his life in politics or war. Instead, a partial solar eclipse he witnessed at the age of thirteen set the course of his entire existence. He became convinced that the ability to predict celestial events was the highest form of human knowledge — and he devoted his life to achieving a precision in astronomical observation that the world had never seen before.\n\nKing Frederick II of Denmark shared Tycho\'s obsession, or at least his enthusiasm for spectacular patronage. In 1576, Frederick gave Tycho the island of Hven in the Øresund strait and effectively unlimited funds to build an observatory. What Tycho constructed there was Uraniborg — the Castle of Urania — a purpose-built scientific institution that was the most advanced astronomical facility in the world. For twenty years, Tycho and his team of assistants made nightly observations with instruments of his own design, achieving accuracies ten times greater than any previous astronomer. His naked-eye measurements — made before the invention of the telescope — remain astonishing by any standard.\n\nTycho\'s most famous discovery came in 1572, when he observed a brilliant new star in the constellation Cassiopeia — what we now call a supernova. His careful measurements proved that the object was genuinely among the fixed stars, not a phenomenon of the upper atmosphere as Aristotelian doctrine required. It was a direct challenge to the ancient belief in the immutability of the heavens, and it made Tycho famous across Europe. He followed it with equally precise observations of comets, showing again that they moved through the supposedly unchanging celestial spheres. His cosmological model — the Tychonic system, in which the planets orbit the Sun but the Sun orbits the Earth — was an ingenious attempt to reconcile his observations with his reluctance to fully abandon the Earth-centred universe.\n\nTycho\'s personal life was as dramatic as his science. He lost part of his nose in a duel over a mathematical dispute as a student and wore a metal prosthetic for the rest of his life. He quarrelled furiously with almost everyone who crossed him, including eventually the Danish court, which withdrew his funding after Frederick II died. He ended his days in Prague under the patronage of Emperor Rudolf II, where a young German astronomer named Johannes Kepler joined him as an assistant. When Tycho died in 1601, Kepler inherited his thirty years of precise observational data — and used them to derive the laws of planetary motion that laid the groundwork for Newton\'s universe. Tycho never accepted the heliocentric model he had done so much to make inevitable, but without his data, the scientific revolution would have taken a different, slower path."
